1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,610
She had spent twenty minutes with git add
-p, staging exactly the hunks, the blocks

2
00:00:09,650 --> 00:00:14,761
of changed lines, that belonged in the next
commit, and leaving the debugging lines

3
00:00:14,801 --> 00:00:19,849
unstaged. Then an interruption, git stash,
which parks uncommitted work and resets

4
00:00:19,889 --> 00:00:23,076
the files. A branch switch,
and later git stash pop.

5
00:00:23,116 --> 00:00:28,102
Every change came back as unstaged. The
separation she had built is gone from git

6
00:00:28,142 --> 00:00:28,536
status.

7
00:00:28,876 --> 00:00:34,104
The short answer for the CTO is: nothing
was lost. A stash entry is a commit with

8
00:00:34,144 --> 00:00:39,567
two or three parents, and the second parent
holds the index, the staging area, as it

9
00:00:39,607 --> 00:00:44,640
was. A plain git stash pop ignores it.
And pop printed the ID of the commit it

10
00:00:44,680 --> 00:00:45,160
dropped.

11
00:00:45,500 --> 00:00:49,827
To give that answer with confidence you
have to know what a stash entry is made of.

12
00:00:51,667 --> 00:00:55,411
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

13
00:00:55,451 --> 00:01:00,375
You learned the stash commands in the undo
module: push, list, apply, pop, drop.

14
00:01:00,415 --> 00:01:05,525
In the recovery module you rescued a dropped
entry by treating it as an unreachable

15
00:01:05,565 --> 00:01:07,758
merge commit. Today you open one up.

16
00:01:08,098 --> 00:01:13,285
This chapter has a theme that will come
back in every video of this module.

17
00:01:13,325 --> 00:01:19,139
A repository holds content that is versioned
and travels with clone, fetch and push.

18
00:01:19,179 --> 00:01:24,713
And it holds machinery that is local and
never travels: .git/config, .git/hooks,

19
00:01:24,753 --> 00:01:29,661
.git/rr-cache, refs/stash. The stash
is on the local side of that line.

20
00:01:31,501 --> 00:01:34,811
draw the commits of a stash entry made
with and without untracked files;

21
00:01:35,151 --> 00:01:39,127
say which tree holds the staged
state and which the unstaged state;

22
00:01:39,467 --> 00:01:42,301
show that the stash list
is the reflog of one ref;

23
00:01:42,641 --> 00:01:46,009
create a stash commit without
touching the list, and store it;

24
00:01:46,349 --> 00:01:48,199
recover a dropped entry by ID.

25
00:01:50,039 --> 00:01:54,667
In one sentence: a stash entry is a
commit W that records your tracked files.

26
00:01:54,707 --> 00:01:59,517
Its first parent is H, the commit that
was HEAD. Its second parent is a commit I

27
00:01:59,557 --> 00:02:04,610
that records the index. And when you stash
untracked files, a parentless commit U is

28
00:02:04,650 --> 00:02:08,369
its third. A parent is the commit
another commit was built on.

29
00:02:08,709 --> 00:02:13,988
refs/stash points at the newest W, and the
stash list is the reflog of that ref: the

30
00:02:14,028 --> 00:02:16,331
local list of values the ref has had.

31
00:02:16,671 --> 00:02:21,241
Precisely, in the manual's words: W is "a
commit whose tree records the state of the

32
00:02:21,281 --> 00:02:25,633
working directory", its first parent is
the commit at HEAD, and "the tree of the

33
00:02:25,673 --> 00:02:30,024
second parent records the state of the
index". The manual doesn't name the third

34
00:02:30,064 --> 00:02:33,976
parent. git stash show --include-untracked
and --only-untracked read it.

35
00:02:34,316 --> 00:02:36,042
Three consequences of the shape.

36
00:02:36,382 --> 00:02:41,173
git stash show -p is the diff from H to W.
The staged and the unstaged part appear

37
00:02:41,213 --> 00:02:41,998
as one change.

38
00:02:42,338 --> 00:02:46,411
git stash apply merges W into
your files with H as the base.

39
00:02:46,451 --> 00:02:51,896
Only --index also reads I. That's the
whole explanation of the hook: a plain pop

40
00:02:51,936 --> 00:02:55,392
restores the files and
doesn't restore the staging.

41
00:02:55,732 --> 00:03:01,742
Revision syntax works on an entry.
stash@{1}^2 is the index commit of the second

42
00:03:01,782 --> 00:03:05,901
entry. stash@{0}:config.yaml
is one file of the newest.

43
00:03:05,941 --> 00:03:09,606
And stash@{n} is reflog
syntax. There is one ref.

44
00:03:09,946 --> 00:03:15,568
Inside the dot git folder: new objects
for W, I and, with -u or -a, U.

45
00:03:15,608 --> 00:03:21,068
The ref refs/stash. And the log
logs/refs/stash, one line per entry.

46
00:03:21,408 --> 00:03:24,604
Two plumbing subcommands
split push into its halves.

47
00:03:24,644 --> 00:03:28,276
git stash create builds the
commits and prints the ID of W.

48
00:03:28,316 --> 00:03:33,379
No ref is written and the files stay as
they are. git stash store puts an existing

49
00:03:33,419 --> 00:03:38,420
stash commit on the list. Scripts use them,
and so does Git: the autostash of git

50
00:03:38,460 --> 00:03:42,589
rebase --autostash is a stash commit
that is never put on the list.

51
00:03:42,929 --> 00:03:47,201
What drop does: it deletes one line
from the log and adjusts the ref.

52
00:03:47,241 --> 00:03:52,013
No object is deleted. The commit is
unreachable until unreachable objects are

53
00:03:52,053 --> 00:03:53,825
pruned, two weeks by default.

54
00:03:54,165 --> 00:03:59,482
And travel: nothing fetches or pushes
refs/stash. Since Git 2.51, git stash export

55
00:03:59,522 --> 00:04:04,643
writes all entries into one chain of
ordinary commits that can be pushed like a

56
00:04:04,683 --> 00:04:08,955
branch, and git stash import turns
such a chain back into entries.

57
00:04:09,295 --> 00:04:14,362
When not to stash: for anything long-lived.
You saw in the recovery module that only

58
00:04:14,402 --> 00:04:18,678
the newest entry is held by a ref.
Long-lived work belongs on a branch.

59
00:04:20,518 --> 00:04:24,570
The textbook's analogy: a labelled
envelope with two or three sheets.

60
00:04:24,610 --> 00:04:29,373
A photocopy of the bench, which is the
files. A photocopy of the outbox, which is

61
00:04:29,413 --> 00:04:33,821
the index. And the loose notes that
were lying around, the untracked files.

62
00:04:34,161 --> 00:04:38,369
The analogy breaks in one place:
there is no drawer for the envelopes.

63
00:04:38,409 --> 00:04:43,102
They are ordinary commits in the object
database, held by one ref and its log.

64
00:04:43,142 --> 00:04:48,199
That's why a dropped stash can be found
again, and also why an expired log loses the

65
00:04:48,239 --> 00:04:48,503
list.

66
00:04:50,343 --> 00:04:55,571
Try it now, on paper, from memory. Draw H,
I, W and U with their parent edges, and

67
00:04:55,611 --> 00:05:00,518
put refs/stash on the right commit. Pause
me for thirty seconds and draw your

68
00:05:00,558 --> 00:05:00,968
answer.

69
00:05:05,808 --> 00:05:10,835
Compare with yours. Without -u, erase U
and its edge: W then has two parents.

70
00:05:10,875 --> 00:05:16,363
In both forms, the difference between H and
I is what was staged, and the difference

71
00:05:16,403 --> 00:05:18,468
between I and W is what was not.

72
00:05:22,808 --> 00:05:27,915
The entry exists. Predict the shape of the
graph: how many parents does the stash

73
00:05:27,955 --> 00:05:30,457
commit have? Say it out loud. I'll wait.

74
00:05:35,297 --> 00:05:39,698
Three. 962e38f is W. In its brackets:
8f8672d, the commit you were on.

75
00:05:39,738 --> 00:05:44,392
Then 7ee160e, which is I, a child of
that commit. And 8fb87d5, which is U.

76
00:05:44,432 --> 00:05:49,467
Look at U's own brackets: empty. It has
no parent. The raw commit shows the same

77
00:05:49,507 --> 00:05:49,848
thing.

78
00:05:50,188 --> 00:05:54,460
An ordinary commit object with
three parent lines. Now the trees.

79
00:05:54,500 --> 00:05:56,980
Compare the blob IDs column by column.

80
00:06:04,120 --> 00:06:08,349
score.py changes between H and
I: 2c9e3f4 becomes 6ba285d.

81
00:06:08,389 --> 00:06:14,090
That's the staged change. config.yaml
changes between I and W: 464dad1 becomes

82
00:06:14,130 --> 00:06:18,948
b571eab. That's the unstaged one.
U holds only the untracked file.

83
00:06:18,988 --> 00:06:24,247
No tree mixes the three kinds of work,
so they can be taken apart again.

84
00:06:27,587 --> 00:06:32,463
git stash show -p prints both tracked
changes together, because it compares H with

85
00:06:32,503 --> 00:06:37,378
W. The two git diff commands at the end of
the snippet separate them, because each

86
00:06:37,418 --> 00:06:39,476
compares the right pair of commits.

87
00:06:39,816 --> 00:06:43,086
Now the ref and its log.
A second entry is pushed.

88
00:06:43,426 --> 00:06:48,428
The ref holds the ID of the newest entry.
The log has one line per entry, oldest

89
00:06:48,468 --> 00:06:53,533
first, in the reflog format you know. git
stash list prints the message column of

90
00:06:53,573 --> 00:06:54,164
this file.

91
00:06:54,504 --> 00:07:00,035
Build an entry without touching anything.
🟢 SAFE: git stash create, then store.

92
00:07:00,375 --> 00:07:06,989
create printed the ID of W, 4bd62aa. The
list didn't change and the file is still

93
00:07:07,029 --> 00:07:11,671
modified. store appended a
reflog line with your message.

94
00:07:11,711 --> 00:07:16,928
The commit's own subject is still
"On main: wip: threshold 0.8".

95
00:07:16,968 --> 00:07:21,692
The list shows the reflog
message, not the commit subject.

96
00:07:22,032 --> 00:07:26,831
Quick quiz, two options. After the
drop, the commit object is deleted.

97
00:07:26,871 --> 00:07:29,320
Or it still exists. Say it out loud.

98
00:07:34,160 --> 00:07:37,608
It still exists. "Dropped
stash@{1}" and the full ID.

99
00:07:37,648 --> 00:07:43,004
The line is gone from the log, and the next
line was rewritten to follow the entry

100
00:07:43,044 --> 00:07:48,400
before it. git cat-file -t still says
commit. That ID, with git stash store or git

101
00:07:48,440 --> 00:07:50,308
stash apply, is the recovery.

102
00:07:50,648 --> 00:07:52,349
Last, stashes that travel.

103
00:07:52,689 --> 00:07:57,145
Each "git stash:" commit has two parents:
the previous commit of the chain and one

104
00:07:57,185 --> 00:08:01,422
stash commit. After a push of that ref
and a fetch elsewhere, git stash import

105
00:08:01,462 --> 00:08:02,409
rebuilds the list.

106
00:08:02,749 --> 00:08:07,854
Imported entries show their commit subjects,
because messages such as "stored by

107
00:08:07,894 --> 00:08:12,034
hand" exist only in the reflog of
the repository that wrote them.

108
00:08:13,874 --> 00:08:18,072
Five mistakes to watch for. Almost
everyone makes the first one.

109
00:08:18,412 --> 00:08:22,138
Expecting git stash pop to
restore what was staged.

110
00:08:22,178 --> 00:08:28,342
Root cause: apply and pop merge W with H as
the base; only --index also reads I, the

111
00:08:28,382 --> 00:08:29,376
second parent.

112
00:08:29,716 --> 00:08:33,277
Reading git stash show -p
as "what I had unstaged".

113
00:08:33,317 --> 00:08:39,067
Root cause: it is the diff from H to W, so
the staged and unstaged parts appear as

114
00:08:39,107 --> 00:08:39,844
one change.

115
00:08:40,184 --> 00:08:45,063
Treating a dropped stash as deleted. Root
cause: drop removes a reflog line; the

116
00:08:45,103 --> 00:08:48,999
commits stay in the object database,
unreachable, until a prune.

117
00:08:49,339 --> 00:08:54,928
Expecting stashes on another machine after
a push. Root cause: nothing fetches or

118
00:08:54,968 --> 00:09:00,141
pushes refs/stash; export and import
exist since Git 2.51 for that purpose.

119
00:09:00,481 --> 00:09:03,517
Searching for a stash by the
message shown in the list.

120
00:09:03,557 --> 00:09:08,102
Root cause: the list prints the reflog
message, which can differ from the commit's

121
00:09:08,142 --> 00:09:08,549
subject.

122
00:09:10,389 --> 00:09:14,777
Now, out of the lab, and back to the
engineer whose staging disappeared.

123
00:09:14,817 --> 00:09:19,881
Her terminal still shows the line that pop
printed: "Dropped refs/stash@{0}" and an

124
00:09:19,921 --> 00:09:24,924
ID. The lead asks her to change nothing
else and reads the dropped commit's second

125
00:09:24,964 --> 00:09:28,061
parent: that tree is the
index as she had built it.

126
00:09:28,101 --> 00:09:32,980
The staged hunks are restored from it,
and the debugging lines stay unstaged, as

127
00:09:33,020 --> 00:09:33,595
they were.

128
00:09:33,935 --> 00:09:37,596
The team's note afterwards is
two lines from the textbook.

129
00:09:37,636 --> 00:09:42,062
When the staging mattered, bring work
back with git stash pop --index.

130
00:09:42,102 --> 00:09:47,422
And when you need one file from an entry,
read it with git show on the entry and the

131
00:09:47,462 --> 00:09:52,016
path, or git restore --source from
the entry, and leave the entry alone.

132
00:09:53,856 --> 00:09:57,813
Your turn. Do Lab 14.6,
"Stash anatomy", in

133
00:09:57,853 --> 00:10:01,717
lab-manual/m14-hooks-rerere-attributes.md.

134
00:10:02,057 --> 00:10:06,603
Before you look at the graph of your
entry, draw it: how many commits, which

135
00:10:06,643 --> 00:10:11,491
parents, which one has none. Before each
git ls-tree, predict which blob IDs will

136
00:10:11,531 --> 00:10:16,319
differ from the previous listing. In the
recovery part, predict what a plain pop

137
00:10:16,359 --> 00:10:18,371
will restore and what it will not.

138
00:10:18,711 --> 00:10:25,122
The challenge is Exercise 14.4, Level 2,
draw the graph, "The shape of a stash

139
00:10:25,162 --> 00:10:29,671
entry", in
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

140
00:10:31,511 --> 00:10:34,985
Pause, draw it on paper, and
answer out loud. I'll wait.

141
00:10:39,825 --> 00:10:44,657
A strong answer draws four commits with
the right parent edges and says which one

142
00:10:44,697 --> 00:10:49,649
has no parent and why that is unusual. It
locates each kind of work as a difference

143
00:10:49,689 --> 00:10:54,580
between two named trees, not as "in the
stash". It states the pair of commits that

144
00:10:54,620 --> 00:10:59,271
show -p compares, and from that explains
a behavior the interviewer didn't ask

145
00:10:59,311 --> 00:11:04,323
about, such as what a plain pop restores.
It also says what holds the entry: one ref

146
00:11:04,363 --> 00:11:04,925
and a log.

147
00:11:06,765 --> 00:11:09,692
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

148
00:11:10,032 --> 00:11:14,695
A stash entry is a merge commit W with
parents H and I, and a third parentless

149
00:11:14,735 --> 00:11:17,288
parent U when untracked
files are included.

150
00:11:17,628 --> 00:11:22,135
The staged change is the difference between
H and I; the unstaged change is the

151
00:11:22,175 --> 00:11:23,689
difference between I and W.

152
00:11:24,029 --> 00:11:28,610
refs/stash names the newest entry and
the stash list is that ref's reflog.

153
00:11:28,950 --> 00:11:33,879
git stash create builds an entry without
touching anything, and git stash store

154
00:11:33,919 --> 00:11:35,263
lists an existing one.

155
00:11:35,603 --> 00:11:40,600
A dropped entry is still a commit; with
its ID I can store or apply it until it is

156
00:11:40,640 --> 00:11:41,030
pruned.

157
00:11:42,870 --> 00:11:46,277
Read section 14C.2 of Chapter 14C.

158
00:11:46,617 --> 00:11:50,589
Today you opened a stash entry and
found ordinary commits inside.

159
00:11:50,629 --> 00:11:55,465
Before the next video, stash something
in the lab and draw its graph before you

160
00:11:55,505 --> 00:11:59,662
print it. Next time: rerere, which
lets you resolve a conflict once.

161
00:11:59,702 --> 00:12:02,871
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

162
00:12:02,911 --> 00:12:04,353
See you in the next one.
